 ## Overview
-The Staggered Optimization Scheme decomposes the coupled optimization of shape (mesh geometry) and Point Spread Function (PSF) parameters into two sequential, alternating sub-problems to manage computational cost and avoid bias [S1, unofficial]. Unlike methods that optimize all parameters simultaneously, this approach iterates between a shape update step using an approximate, narrow-band Fourier Transform (FT) and a PSF update step using an exact, full-spectrum FT [S1, unofficial]. It is specifically designed for differentiable rendering in 3D fluorescence microscopy where the forward pass is significantly slower than the backward pass, and where the narrow-band approximation introduces bias dependent on the current PSF state [S1, unofficial].
+The Staggered Optimization Scheme is a training procedure for differentiable volume rendering that decouples the joint optimization of geometric shape parameters and Point Spread Function (PSF) parameters into two sequential sub-problems [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of optimization instability and computational bias caused by the strong coupling between shape and PSF, where optimizing them simultaneously can lead to degenerate shapes or incorrect blur estimation [S1, unofficial]. Unlike joint optimization methods that update all parameters in a single step, this scheme alternates between a shape optimization phase and a PSF optimization phase, allowing each to converge more stably [S1, unofficial]. It is specifically designed for differentiable mesh-based rendering in fluorescence microscopy, where the forward pass is significantly slower than the backward pass [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on a nonmanifold multimaterial mesh representation with $n_v$ vertices (typically 1,000–10,000) and a Gaussian isotropic PSF defined by a covariance matrix $\Sigma$ [S1, unofficial]. The optimization alternates between minimizing a shape-specific loss and a PSF-specific loss, iterating until convergence [S1, unofficial].
+The method operates on a nonmanifold multimaterial mesh representation of the scene, typically containing 1,000–10,000 vertices [S1, unofficial]. The optimization iterates between two distinct steps: Shape Optimization and PSF Optimization. The core mechanism relies on the differentiability of the mesh Fourier Transform (FT) computation, which serves as the forward rendering operator in this context [S1, unofficial].
 
